import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj23.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. The Core Principle — All R&amp;D Expensed as Incurred" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. No Development-Stage Capitalisation Option — Unlike IAS 38" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. The Software Cost Carve-Out" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. What This \"Partial Amendment\" Refined" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Worked Example — Expensing R&amp;D Costs in Full" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — the Software Carve-Out in Practice" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
<div class="hub">
  <div class="hub-box">
    <div class="hub-title">ASBJ Statement No. 23 — Partial Amendments to Accounting Standard for Research and Development Costs</div>
    <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan GAAP (J-GAAP) · 研究開発費等に係る会計基準の一部改正 · The most conservative R&D treatment among the major frameworks · JPY examples</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="toc"><a href="#s1">1. Conceptual Framework Linkage</a><a href="#s2">2. Scope</a><a href="#s3">3. The Core Principle — All R&amp;D Expensed as Incurred</a><a href="#s4">4. No Development-Stage Capitalisation Option — Unlike IAS 38</a><a href="#s5">5. The Software Cost Carve-Out</a><a href="#s6">6. What This "Partial Amendment" Refined</a><a href="#s7">7. Worked Example — Expensing R&amp;D Costs in Full</a><a href="#s8">8. Worked Example — the Software Carve-Out in Practice</a><a href="#s9">9. Disclosure Requirements</a><a href="#s10">10. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</a><a href="#s11">11. Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP</a><a href="#s12">12. Statement Mapping</a><a href="#s13">13. Related Standards</a><a href="#s14">14. Glossary</a></div>
<div class="section-header" id="s1">
  <span class="num">1</span>
  <h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">A deliberately simple, conservative principle — expense everything, immediately</div>
    <div class="principle">J-GAAP's research and development accounting rests on a GENUINELY SIMPLE, foundational principle: ALL research and development COSTS — WHETHER "research" or "development" in nature — are EXPENSED AS INCURRED, WITHOUT any capitalisation option based on meeting specific development-stage criteria. ASBJ Statement No. 23 represents a "PARTIAL AMENDMENT" REFINING and CLARIFYING the application of this CORE principle (established under the earlier foundational Japanese R&D standard), rather than changing the underlying principle itself.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s2">
  <span class="num">2</span>
  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">All companies incurring research and development costs</div>
    <div class="principle">This Statement applies to ALL companies incurring RESEARCH and DEVELOPMENT costs — expenditure aimed at the DISCOVERY of new knowledge (research) or the APPLICATION of research findings to a plan or design for new/improved products or processes (development).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s3">
  <span class="num">3</span>
  <h2>The Core Principle — All R&D Expensed as Incurred</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">No distinction between research and development for capitalisation purposes</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">UNLIKE some other frameworks that distinguish "RESEARCH" from "DEVELOPMENT" for capitalisation PURPOSES, J-GAAP treats BOTH categories THE SAME WAY — ALL research and development costs are recognised as an EXPENSE in the Statement of Income IMMEDIATELY as incurred, REGARDLESS of how far along the development process an activity may be, or how LIKELY future economic benefits may seem.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s4">
  <span class="num">4</span>
  <h2>No Development-Stage Capitalisation Option — Unlike IAS 38</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">The most conservative treatment among the major accounting frameworks</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">IAS 38 REQUIRES capitalisation of DEVELOPMENT costs (though NOT research costs) once SPECIFIC criteria are met (technical feasibility, intention and ability to complete, probable future economic benefit, reliably measurable costs). Even FRS 102 (UK GAAP) offers development cost capitalisation as a genuine POLICY CHOICE (see the companion FRS 102 poster). J-GAAP offers<strong>NEITHER</strong>a MANDATORY NOR an ELECTIVE capitalisation option for development costs — the GENERAL rule is IMMEDIATE expensing, FULL STOP, making J-GAAP's treatment of R&D genuinely the MOST CONSERVATIVE and SIMPLEST among the major frameworks surveyed in this poster series.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s5">
  <span class="num">5</span>
  <h2>The Software Cost Carve-Out</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">A narrow exception for software development costs, addressed separately</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">A NARROW exception exists for CERTAIN software development costs — where software is developed for EXTERNAL sale/lease, or for internal use, SEPARATE practical guidance permits CAPITALISATION of qualifying costs INCURRED after specific criteria are met (broadly PARALLELING how the US GAAP ASC 985/ASC 350-40 framework CARVES software costs OUT of the general R&D expensing principle under ASC 730 — see the companion ASC 985 poster). This CARVE-OUT is genuinely NARROW, however — it does NOT represent a general development-cost capitalisation option for R&D costs OUTSIDE the software context.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s6">
  <span class="num">6</span>
  <h2>What This "Partial Amendment" Refined</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Clarifying scope and application, without altering the core expensing principle</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">This Statement REPRESENTS a "PARTIAL AMENDMENT" to Japan's foundational R&D cost standard — REFINING and CLARIFYING specific application questions (such as the PRECISE scope of what constitutes "research" or "development" activity, and how the standard interacts with SOFTWARE-specific guidance) WITHOUT altering the UNDERLYING, foundational "expense as incurred" principle itself, which has remained a CONSISTENT, STABLE feature of Japanese accounting practice.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s7">
  <span class="num">7</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — Expensing R&D Costs in Full</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">No capitalisation, regardless of the project's stage</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: A pharmaceutical company's late-stage drug development</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A pharmaceutical company incurs ¥400,000,000 on late-stage clinical trials for a promising new drug candidate, with regulatory approval considered highly likely based on results to date.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the ADVANCED stage and HIGH likelihood of success, the ENTIRE ¥400,000,000 is<strong>EXPENSED IMMEDIATELY</strong>under J-GAAP — there is NO capitalisation option, UNLIKE how an ENTITY reporting under IAS 38 might potentially CAPITALISE SOME of these late-stage development costs if the SPECIFIC IAS 38 criteria were met.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s8">
  <span class="num">8</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — the Software Carve-Out in Practice</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">A narrow exception, not a general rule change</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: Software developed for external sale</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A software company incurs ¥150,000,000 developing a commercial software product, AFTER technological feasibility has been established (per the narrow software-specific guidance).</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">This ¥150,000,000 MAY be CAPITALISED under the SPECIFIC software cost guidance CARVE-OUT — but this remains a NARROW EXCEPTION specific to SOFTWARE; the SAME company's OTHER, non-software R&D costs (e.g. hardware component research) would CONTINUE to be expensed IMMEDIATELY under the GENERAL rule.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s9">
  <span class="num">9</span>
  <h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 23 requires</div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>Total amount of research and development costs expensed during the period</span><span>Classification of R&amp;D expense within the Statement of Income (e.g. cost of sales or SG&amp;A)</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s10">
  <span class="num">10</span>
  <h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a cost genuinely qualifies as R&amp;D expenditure versus another cost category</span><span>Whether the narrow software cost carve-out applies to a specific expenditure</span></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — the immediate expensing principle avoids most estimation complexity</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s11">
  <span class="num">11</span>
  <h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison across all three major frameworks</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-1-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 23)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 38) / US GAAP (ASC 730)</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Research costs</span><span>Expensed as incurred</span><span>Expensed as incurred — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-1-1-1"><span>Development costs</span><span>Expensed as incurred — NO capitalisation option</span><span>IFRS: MANDATORY capitalisation once criteria met. US GAAP: generally expensed (similar to J-GAAP), with narrow exceptions</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Software development costs</span><span>Narrow carve-out permits capitalisation</span><span>Both frameworks carve out software costs from the general R&amp;D principle — converged in APPROACH, if not in every mechanical detail</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">On GENERAL (non-software) R&D costs, J-GAAP is actually CLOSER to US GAAP's general expensing approach than to IAS 38's mandatory development cost capitalisation — a notable case where J-GAAP, US GAAP, and IFRS form a THREE-WAY comparison rather than a simple two-sided divergence.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s12">
  <span class="num">12</span>
  <h2>Statement Mapping</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Where every R&D cost event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>R&amp;D cost incurred</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Expensed immediately, with a narrow software carve-out</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s13">
  <span class="num">13</span>
  <h2>Related Standards</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 18</span><span>Asset Retirement Obligations — a separate area where J-GAAP is instead genuinely converged with international practice, illustrating the topic-by-topic variability of J-GAAP's convergence position</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s14">
  <span class="num">14</span>
  <h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item">
      <div class="glossary-term">Research and development costs (研究開発費)</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">Expenditure on the discovery of new knowledge (research) or applying findings to new/improved products or processes (development), expensed as incurred under J-GAAP.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
      <div class="glossary-term">Software cost carve-out</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">A narrow exception permitting capitalisation of certain qualifying software development costs, separate from the general R&D expensing rule.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="legend">
  <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
  <div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span>Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)<span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span>Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)<span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span>純資産の部<span class="badge be">SCNA</span>Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)<span class="badge bc">SCF</span>Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div>
</div>
<div class="footer-note">ASBJ Statement No. 23 — Partial Amendments to Accounting Standard for Research and Development Costs · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
